---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const crumbs = [{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'Moderation' }];

const REJECTED = [
	'Any statement of whether a claim should have been paid, however it is phrased and whoever writes it.',
	'Any naming of an individual adjuster, agent or employee. Name the carrier or the department; never the person.',
	'Any personal information about a third party, including anyone on the other side of the situation.',
	'Any document upload. There is no mechanism to accept one, which is the only reliable way to enforce this.',
	'Any premium, quote or rate claim, including what somebody was charged.',
	'Any ranking of carriers, or any statement of which is best, cheapest or most reliable.',
	'Anything presented as a client result of any brokerage.',
];
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Moderated before publication, not after"
	eyebrow="Moderation"
	lede="A queue, not a report button. It is slower than the alternative and it is the entire product: unmoderated volume is exactly what makes a forum uncitable."
	crumbs={crumbs}
>
	<section class="band band-tight">
		<div class="shell page-grid">
			<div class="prose">
				<h2>Why before rather than after</h2>
				<p>
					Almost every discussion site moderates after the fact: anyone may post, and problems are
					removed once somebody flags them. That model optimises for volume, and volume is not what
					this site is for. If a reader cannot trust that what they are reading was checked, the
					archive is worth no more than any other forum, and the effort of structuring it was
					wasted.
				</p>
				<p>
					So nothing appears here until it has been read. The consequence is honest and worth
					stating plainly: <strong>this will be slow, and it will stay small for a long time.</strong>{' '}
					That is the trade being made deliberately.
				</p>

				<h2>What is rejected outright</h2>
				<ul>
					{REJECTED.map((r) => <li>{r}</li>)}
				</ul>

				<h2>What happens to an account that is nearly publishable</h2>
				<p>
					Most submissions will not be rejected; they will be incomplete. An account with no date,
					no state, or no record of who decided cannot be published as it stands, and the fix is
					usually two questions back to the person who wrote it.
				</p>
				<ol>
					<li>
						<strong>Identifying detail is removed before publication, not after.</strong> Names,
						addresses, policy numbers, employer names where they are not the point, and anything
						that identifies a third party.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>The label is set by the moderator, not by the contributor.</strong> A person
						describing their own situation is not always the best judge of what kind of evidence it
						is, and the label is the field a reader relies on most.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>The reasons it does not generalise are written or expanded.</strong> Three is
						the floor. Most real accounts have more.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Anything reading as a verdict is removed</strong> — not softened. Making a
						prohibited statement vaguer leaves it in place while hiding it, which is worse than
						either publishing or cutting it.
					</li>
				</ol>

				<h2>Who moderates</h2>
				<p>
					At launch, the same licensed reviewer who signs off the Record. That is a real constraint
					on throughput and it is why the sequence matters: reports first, practitioner notes
					second, open discussion last. Each stage is only worth opening once the one before it is
					worth reading.
				</p>

				<h2>Withdrawal</h2>
				<p>
					<strong>A contributor may withdraw a report at any time, for any reason or none.</strong>{' '}
					Nothing asks them to justify taking their own account back, and there is no step that
					argues with them about it. Anything not yet published goes on the spot; a published
					report is a file, so it is removed the same day by the moderator who committed it.
				</p>
				<p>
					A withdrawn report keeps its address and says that it was withdrawn. It is not deleted,
					because a citation that silently 404s is worse for the person who relied on it than one
					that explains itself.
				</p>
				<p class="meta">
					Withdrawing a report does not close the account it was submitted from, and closing an
					account is a separate thing we will build when somebody asks for it.
				</p>

				<h2>Corrections</h2>
				<p>
					If a published report is wrong, it is corrected with the prior wording kept visible, the
					same way the Record handles it. A record of having been wrong is worth more than an
					unblemished one, because only the first is checkable.
				</p>
			</div>

			<aside aria-label="Related">
				<div class="rail-block">
					<h2>The rule</h2>
					<p style="font-size: var(--text-sm)">
						Nobody publishes a view on whether a claim should have been paid. It is the only rule
						that crosses from the Record, and it is enforced here by reading rather than by a
						build step.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="rail-block">
					<h2>Related</h2>
					<ul>
						<li><a href="/standards">Standards</a></li>
						<li><a href="/contribute">Contribute</a></li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</aside>
		</div>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
